[287.319, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ The gravity well now curls nearby matter into a vortex without blowing out. Cleaning up test files.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[288.003, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(rm -rf <workspace>/.shots && ls -la <workspace>)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[296.119, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I built it as a single page, **`<workspace>/index.html`**. Open it in a browser; it loads three.js from a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  CDN, so it needs an internet connection.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I checked it in headless Chrome by taking screenshots at specific moments in the loop and with simulated\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  mouse input. I haven't watched it run in real time, so I haven't confirmed how smooth it is on real\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  hardware.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The 10-second loop:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **0–3.4s, the solar system:** A white-hot sun and seven neon planets (cyan, magenta, lime, orange, a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  violet ringed giant, blue and red) orbit inside glowing trails of orbit dust. There's also a violet/cyan\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  asteroid belt, an outer nebula halo and twinkling stars.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **3.4–6.8s, the implosion:** Everything spirals inward, speeding up and shifting toward hot pink and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  white. The system flattens into a churning disc, a colour-cycling neon ring shrinks in from the edges, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the camera moves in.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **About 7.15s, the collapse:** All matter reaches a point, then a bright flash with camera shake.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **7.15–10s, the explosion:** Matter bursts outward, overshoots a little, and settles back into orbit. Two\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  neon shockwave rings spread out at different tilts.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The loop is seamless because every motion repeats exactly every 10 seconds, so the last frame matches the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  first. The progress bar in the bottom-left tracks the cycle.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Mouse and touch:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Moving the pointer** pushes particles aside and swirls them in a glowing wake, and tilts the camera\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  slightly toward the pointer.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Holding the button down** turns the pointer into a gravity well that pulls nearby matter into a vortex\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  around it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The motion is calculated on the graphics card for about 120,000 particles. Glow and faint motion trails are\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  added on top.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  To check a single moment, add `?t=` with a fraction of the loop to the address, for example\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `index.html?t=0.715` to freeze on the collapse flash.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
